{
  "name": "WorkMate.StandardLookupLists",
  "displayName": "WorkMate standard lookup lists",
  "description": "The managed lists a form field offers as choices: job title, grade, employment type, work location, document type, nationality, country, currency, relationship and contact type. Safe to apply to a tenant that already has them.",
  "author": "Aramis Enterprise Solutions",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "issetuprecipe": false,
  "categories": ["workmate"],
  "tags": ["workmate", "lookup-lists"],

  "$notes": [
    "Applied by base.recipe.json on every new tenant, and applied by hand to a tenant created before slice B2. Those are the same file on purpose: a tenant that gets the lists at setup and one that gets them later must end up with the same lists, and two files would drift.",
    "Re-running is safe and is the point. Per ADR-0008's lookup-list addendum: an entry this file names that the tenant does not hold is ADDED, an entry that exists and differs FAILS the step naming the difference, and entries the tenant has added of its own are left alone. So a later version of this file can add an entry without an administrator retyping it, and cannot quietly revert one they renamed.",
    "Codes are the things records store, so they are chosen to be what a customer already recognises: ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 for countries and nationalities, ISO 4217 for currencies. Changing one later is not possible - an entry's code is immutable once saved - so they are worth getting right here.",
    "Entries are deliberately a starting point rather than an exhaustive set. A customer adds their own through People > Forms > Lookup lists, and re-applying this file leaves those alone.",
    "Arabic is present on every entry here because these are terms WorkMate ships and can translate. A customer's own entries may have English only; ADR-0003's addendum makes Arabic optional unless the tenant turns RequireArabicNames on."
  ],

